---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../../../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import StylingHooks from "../../../components/StylingHooks.astro";
import { SidebarDemo } from "../../../components/react-demos/sidebar";



const install = `pnpm add @skryensya/core @skryensya/vanilla`;

const css = `@import "@skryensya/core/tokens.scss";
@import "@skryensya/core/components/sidebar.css";
@import "@skryensya/core/patterns/nav-list.css";
@import "@skryensya/core/patterns/icon.css";
@import "@skryensya/core/patterns/state-layer.css";`;

const html = `<aside
  class="sk-sidebar"
  data-sk-sidebar
  id="app-sidebar"
>
  <div class="sk-sidebar__header">
    <button
      class="sk-sidebar__trigger sk-interactive"
      data-sk-sidebar-trigger
      type="button"
      aria-label="Collapse navigation"
    >
      <span data-sk-icon="menu"></span>
    </button>
  </div>

  <div class="sk-sidebar__content" data-sk-sidebar-content>
    <!-- The sidebar is the shell; the list is a pattern,
         invited here -->
    <nav class="sk-nav-list" aria-label="Principal">
      <div class="sk-nav-list__group">
        <div class="sk-nav-list__group-label" id="app-workspace">
          Espacio
        </div>
        <ul class="sk-nav-list__list" aria-labelledby="app-workspace">
          <li class="sk-nav-list__item">
            <a
              class="sk-nav-list__link sk-interactive"
              href="/"
              aria-current="page"
            >
              <span data-sk-icon="info"></span>
              <span class="sk-nav-list__label">Home</span>
            </a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </nav>
  </div>

  <hr class="sk-sidebar__separator" />
  <div class="sk-sidebar__footer">v0.3.0</div>
</aside>`;

const js = `import { initComponents } from "@skryensya/vanilla/auto";
import { mountIcons } from "@skryensya/vanilla/icon";
import { lucideIcons } from "@skryensya/icons-lucide";

// Icons are authored as <span data-sk-icon> placeholders;
// mountIcons replaces them with the <svg> of the linked set.
mountIcons(document, lucideIcons);

await initComponents();`;

const react = `import { Icon } from "@skryensya/react/icon";
import {
  NavList,
  NavListGroup,
  NavListLink,
} from "@skryensya/react/nav-list";
import {
  Sidebar,
  SidebarContent,
  SidebarFooter,
  SidebarHeader,
  SidebarTrigger,
} from "@skryensya/react/sidebar";

function Example() {
  return (
    <Sidebar
      defaultCollapsed={false}
      onCollapsedChange={({ collapsed }) => SomeFunction(collapsed)}
    >
      <SidebarHeader>
        <SidebarTrigger
          label="Collapse navigation"
          icon={<Icon name="menu" />}
        />
      </SidebarHeader>

      <SidebarContent>
        <NavList aria-label="Principal">
          <NavListGroup label="Espacio">
            <NavListLink href="/" icon={<Icon name="info" />} current>
              Home
            </NavListLink>
            <NavListLink
              href="/reportes"
              icon={<Icon name="calendar" />}
              trailing="12"
            >
              Reportes
            </NavListLink>
          </NavListGroup>
        </NavList>
      </SidebarContent>

      <SidebarFooter>v0.3.0</SidebarFooter>
    </Sidebar>
  );
}`;

---

<Base title="Sidebar" description="Sidebar: The shell that collapses to a rail, with the navigation list as a guest pattern.">
  <h1>Sidebar</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Sidebar is the <strong>shell</strong>: a header, a scrolling middle, a footer, and the collapse.
    It contracts to a <strong>rail</strong>, narrows, never hides.
  </p>

  <h2>The sidebar is one thing; another, its content</h2>
  <p>
    What goes inside is not a matter of the sidebar. The destination list is the pattern <a href="/en/nav-list"><code>nav-list</code></a>, invited here and with the same structure that the navbar uses horizontally. That is why there is no <code>sk-sidebar__link</code>: that list was never in the sidebar, and naming it like that would be naming a tenant.
  </p>

  <p>Collapse the sidebar with the button: the list becomes a rail of icons, and it is all still there.</p>
  {/* The slot, not the `html` prop: a sidebar is only legible next to the thing it sits beside, and
      that surrounding shell is the site playing consumer, not part of the markup being taught. */}
  <ComponentPreview code={html} react={react} label="Sidebar" flush>
    <div class="app-shell">
      <Fragment set:html={html} />
      <div class="app-shell__main">
        <p>The content of the app goes here.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <SidebarDemo client:load />
    </Fragment>
  </ComponentPreview>

  <h2>Why is it not a <code>&lt;details&gt;</code></h2>
  <p>
    A disclosure is sent by the platform: <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;summary&gt;</code>, without machine. A collapsible sidebar <em>not</em> is a disclosure. A closed <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> hides its contents; a collapsed sidebar still shows it, just as icons. Since the platform does not send anything for that, a small enhancer earns its place, and does not reimplement anything that already exists.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is why labels fade out but <strong>not</strong> are removed from the DOM: collapsed, the label is the only thing that names the icon for a screen reader. A nested group that <em>and</em> hides its items is still a disclosure, and goes in <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>One width, two states</h2>
  <p>
    The two widths are two values of <strong>a</strong> styling hook. The consumer writes <code>inline-size: var(--sk-sidebar-inline-size)</code> once; collapsing re-declares that hook does not add a new one, which is the rule of styling hooks. <code>state</code> is written by the machine as <code>data-state</code> in the root, never by hand.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rail measures exactly one control, and when the sidebar collapses it passes the list the
    padding that <em>centers</em> the icon in that square. The value is calculated rather than
    guessed, so it stays centered at all three densities. The shell re-declares a pattern hook; it
    never touches the list markup.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is also no <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> block: duration comes from the expand and
    collapse intent tokens, which already shorten themselves. The sidebar lends that intent to the
    list, so labels fade during the same interval in which its width changes.
  </p>

  <h2>The trigger does not have a visible label</h2>
  <p>
    It measures one icon wide, so its accessible name goes in a <code>aria-label</code> (or in visually hidden text): a visible label inside would be text inside a square the width of an icon. The enhancer patches attributes, never content, the name is yours.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="terminal" />

  <h2>CSS</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={css} label="CSS" />

  <h2>Authored HTML</h2>
  <p>
    The enhancer looks for <code>[data-sk-sidebar]</code>, demands a <code>[data-sk-sidebar-trigger]</code>, and patches <code>aria-expanded</code>, <code>aria-controls</code>, and <code>data-state</code>. Does not write markup or classes. The full markup is in the <strong>Vanilla</strong> tab of the preview.
  </p>

  <h2>Initialize vanilla</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={js} label="JavaScript" />

  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>
    Controlled (<code>collapsed</code>) or uncontrolled (<code>defaultCollapsed</code>), with <code>onCollapsedChange</code> to persist the preference. The equivalent event in vanilla is <code>sk-collapsed-change</code>. Persistence is up to the consumer: with what password and by user or by device is not decided by a design system. The code is in the <strong>React</strong> tab of the preview.
  </p>
  <StylingHooks groups={[{"file":"components/sidebar.css","probe":"sk-sidebar"}]} />
</Base>
